1
00:00:00,165 --> 00:00:01,055
<u>Jetpack Compose</u>

2
00:00:02,393 --> 00:00:03,663
<i>What is Jetpack Compose?</i>

3
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:07,590
Jetpack Compose is a declarative UI toolkit for Android.

4
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:12,510
UI is described as a function of state, and Compose updates the affected parts when that state changes.

5
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:16,700
<i>Which basic Compose containers are used to arrange elements?</i>

6
00:00:18,219 --> 00:00:22,279
Row arranges elements horizontally, Column vertically, and Box allows elements to overlap.

7
00:00:22,979 --> 00:00:27,099
For large scrollable collections, Compose provides LazyColumn, LazyRow, and lazy grids.

8
00:00:28,609 --> 00:00:30,779
<i>Which stability annotations exist in Compose?</i>

9
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:34,509
The main annotations are @Immutable and @Stable.

10
00:00:35,209 --> 00:00:38,849
They are contracts with the Compose compiler: @Immutable promises immutable public state,

11
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:41,969
while @Stable promises predictable and observable property changes.

12
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:46,040
<i>Are lambdas without captured variables stable in Compose?</i>

13
00:00:47,555 --> 00:00:50,955
Usually, a non-capturing lambda is reused and treated as a stable value.

14
00:00:51,655 --> 00:00:53,945
A lambda that captures mutable data or is recreated

15
00:00:53,965 --> 00:00:56,635
can change identity and prevent recomposition from being skipped.

16
00:00:58,142 --> 00:00:59,822
<i>What are the rendering phases in Compose?</i>

17
00:01:01,347 --> 00:01:03,727
The main phases are composition, layout, and drawing.

18
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:09,177
Composition defines the UI structure, layout measures and positions elements, and drawing renders their content.

19
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:13,300
<i>Which structure stores the Compose composition tree and state?</i>

20
00:01:14,827 --> 00:01:16,917
Internally, Compose uses the Slot Table.

21
00:01:17,617 --> 00:01:22,367
The Composer stores composition groups, remember values, and data required for future recompositions there.

22
00:01:23,897 --> 00:01:24,977
<i>What is recomposition?</i>

23
00:01:26,507 --> 00:01:30,677
Recomposition is the repeated execution of composable functions that depend on changed state.

24
00:01:31,377 --> 00:01:35,487
It is a normal Compose mechanism; it becomes a problem only when it is too frequent or expensive.

25
00:01:37,353 --> 00:01:39,663
<i>What is the difference between remember and rememberSaveable?</i>

26
00:01:40,922 --> 00:01:44,422
remember keeps a value across recompositions but does not survive recreation.

27
00:01:45,122 --> 00:01:48,432
rememberSaveable also stores supported state through the saved state mechanism.

28
00:01:49,945 --> 00:01:51,065
<i>What is mutableStateOf?</i>

29
00:01:52,579 --> 00:01:55,279
mutableStateOf creates observable state tracked by Compose.

30
00:01:55,989 --> 00:01:59,889
When its value changes, dependent composable functions may be scheduled for recomposition.

31
00:02:01,409 --> 00:02:02,459
<i>What is state hoisting?</i>

32
00:02:03,995 --> 00:02:06,725
State hoisting means moving state higher in the composable tree.

33
00:02:07,435 --> 00:02:11,755
A component receives a value and callbacks, which makes it more stateless, reusable, and testable.

34
00:02:13,281 --> 00:02:14,751
<i>When should LaunchedEffect be used?</i>

35
00:02:16,275 --> 00:02:20,245
LaunchedEffect is used for coroutine side effects tied to the lifecycle of a composable and its key.

36
00:02:20,955 --> 00:02:24,205
When the key changes, the previous coroutine is cancelled and a new one starts.

37
00:02:25,729 --> 00:02:27,669
<i>When should rememberCoroutineScope be used?</i>

38
00:02:29,174 --> 00:02:32,654
It is used to launch a coroutine in response to a UI event, such as a button click.

39
00:02:33,364 --> 00:02:36,304
Screen-level business logic is usually better placed in a ViewModel.

40
00:02:37,817 --> 00:02:40,087
<i>How can unnecessary recompositions be reduced?</i>

41
00:02:41,622 --> 00:02:45,562
Organize state correctly, avoid passing unstable objects without a reason, use stable keys,

42
00:02:45,722 --> 00:02:47,282
and move heavy work out of composables.

43
00:02:47,992 --> 00:02:50,882
Optimization should start with measurement and finding a real bottleneck.
